Although a cell count of 4800 in the aspirate analysis could be seen in both inflammatory and infectious conditions, a very high cell count of >50,000 or even >100,000 is more specific for infectious causes (read the Exam Tip
below). Furthermore, a negative culture does not exclude infection but makes it less likely.
In practice, a cell count of 2000-50,000 is the typical finding in inflammatory joint diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus arthritis, gout, and pseudogout. The absence of crystals, however, makes crystal-induced
inflammation unlikely.
(Option A) Although a traumatic tap results in blood in the synovial fluid, other features such as elevated white cell count are absent unless a concomitant condition is present. A few red blood cells (RBCs) are usually present
even in a normal synovial fluid.
(Option C) In TB arthritis, synovial fluid analysis is usually not helpful. The white cell count can be high or low with the dominance of either neutrophils or lymphocytes and no other specific diagnostic features. In fact, this
aspirate analysis can also be caused by TB as well, yet an inflammatory condition is more likely.
(Option D) Viral arthritis presents with a high white cell count with a predominance of polymorphonuclears (PMNs), but the cultures are negative. 
(Option E) A white cell count of 4100, makes gonococcal arthritis less likely. Gonococcal arthritis often presents with a white cell count of 34000-68000.
 
EXAM TIP
Remember the following tips for the exam:
The WBC count of a normal joint aspirate is <200.
With degenerative diseases (e.g., OA) or trauma, the WBC count of joint fluid is 200-2000.
Typical inflammatory joint fluid either with the crystalline or non-crystalline disease has a WBC of 2000-50,000 (more commonly 5,000-50,000)
The typical WBC count of joint fluid in septic arthritis is usually >50,000; however, septic arthritis should be considered a possibility in every joint aspirate with WBC>5000, especially if the arthritis is monoarticular and
the patient has already received antibiotics.
It is not common for gout and pseudogout to present with WBC>50,000. If so, co-infection is a possibility to consider.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-2578400eed1e","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"9221be17c244340c84b2","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":1147,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"native","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"9221be17c244340c84b2","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_year":2023,"source_page":1147,"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Examination of an aspirate from an acutely painful swollen knee is as follows:\nWhite blood cell count: 4100 (<2000μL)\nRed blood cells: few\nCrystals: negative\nCulture: no growth\nWhich of the following is the most accurate interpretation of the results?","options":{"A":"Traumatic tap.","B":"Inflammation.","C":"TB arthritis.","D":"Viral arthritis.","E":"Gonococcal arthritis."},"source_answer":"B","source_explanation":"Biochemically, the synovial fluid is an ultrafiltrate of plasma across the synovial membrane enriched with various compounds produced by the synoviocytes. In normal physiological conditions, the biochemical composition of\nthe synovial fluid is similar to that of plasma. In pathological conditions, laboratory evaluation of synovial fluid provides information about the pathology of the disease affecting the joint (e.g., arthritis).\nSimilar to other body fluids, common laboratory evaluation of synovial fluid includes the following three steps:\nPhysical examination of synovial fluid (e.g., appearanc